  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Widespread showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and \n  evening, with gusty outflow winds of 30-50 mph, brief moderate \n  rainfall, and lightning, strongest storms south of Highway 200.\n\n- Another round of storms Wednesday capable of 30-50 mph outflows\n  and heavy rain rates up to 0.5 inches per hour.\n\n- Persistent active pattern late week into the weekend under\n  southwesterly flow, with a 20 percent chance of a tropical \n  moisture tap on Sunday or Monday.\n\nAs low pressure off the Pacific northwest coast begins to shift\ninland, a moist southwest flow aloft will transport monsoon\nmoisture from the Desert Southwest into north central Idaho and\nwestern Montana. Showers and thunderstorms will develop through\nnorth central Idaho and southwest Montana between 1 and 3pm MDT\nand shift northwards into the rest of western Montana through 10\npm MDT tonight. With thunderstorms moving into the hot and dry\nairmass already in place, gusty outflow winds will be the main\nthreat. High resolution models generally show gust potential\nbetween 30 to 50 mph with storms. \n\nBy Wednesday, as the low pressure system continues to move\neastwards, a cold front will move through the region, first\nthrough north central Idaho in the morning, then into western\nMontana late morning through early afternoon. Another round of\nthunderstorms is expected to develop ahead of and with the cold\nfront. Storms will be more moist on Wednesday, with stronger\nstorms capable of half an inch of rainfall in an hour or less. \nWith strong forcing aloft, Wednesday's set up is slight better to \nfavor strong to locally severe thunderstorms in the afternoon, \nespecially in northwest Montana near the Continental Divide where \na later frontal passage will correspond with max daytime heating. \nHigh resolution models show localized gusts up to 60 mph will be \npossible. It does appear the threat for strong storms will end \nearlier in the day, possibly as early as 6pm. The good news: There\nis high confidence (80 percent) of widespread wetting rain (0.10 \ninch or greater) through Thursday morning other than for far \nsouthwest Montana.\n\nAcross far southwest Montana gusty winds and low afternoon \nrelative humidity Wednesday afternoon and evening are expected to \ncreate critical fire weather conditions through Wednesday \nafternoon through Thursday afternoon, before the cold front \nfinally passes through Thursday night.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will persist Thursday into Friday as a \nbroad upper-level trough settles over the Pacific Northwest. This \npattern will keep temperatures near to slightly below late-summer \naverages alongside periodic showers and thunderstorms, \nparticularly during peak afternoon and evening heating. Guidance \nremains in solid agreement that troughing will linger over the \nregion through the weekend, with roughly 20 percent of ensemble \nmembers indicating a potential tap of significant tropical \nmoisture Sunday or Monday. Regardless of whether this tropical \nplume materializes, the Northern Rockies will remain embedded in \nan active, unsettled weather pattern through the weekend.",
